1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,840
Many of us love live music – listening to the singer belting out classics we love to sing along to.
我们很多人都喜欢现场演奏的音乐——听着歌手高声演唱那些我们爱跟着哼唱的经典曲目。

2
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:13,660
But while it can be lots of fun nodding our heads to the tunes booming out, it can also harm our hearing.
虽然跟着震耳欲聋的旋律点头晃脑十分有趣，但也会损害我们的听力。

3
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:19,920
Studies suggest that one in ten adults have had their hearing damaged by loud noises or music.
研究表明，十分之一的成年人听力都曾因嘈杂噪音或音乐受到损伤。

4
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:26,620
So why is loud music damaging, and what can we do to prevent it from harming our ears?
那么，为什么喧闹的音乐会造成伤害？我们又能做些什么来保护耳朵呢？

5
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:32,730
Inside our ears, there are lots of tiny hair-like cells located in the cochlea.
我们的耳朵内部，耳蜗里分布着许多微小的毛状细胞。

6
00:00:32,730 --> 00:00:42,750
These cells are referred to as hair cells because very small bundles of stereocilia, which look like hairs under a microscope, sit on top of each cell.
这些细胞被称为毛细胞，是因为每个细胞顶端都有一小束静纤毛，在显微镜下看起来就像头发丝。

7
00:00:42,750 --> 00:00:48,790
These groupings of stereocilia can be damaged when exposed to prolonged loud noises.
如果长期暴露在巨大的噪音中，这些静纤毛束就会受损。

8
00:00:48,790 --> 00:00:58,210
Once damaged, these cells cannot respond to sound, and therefore we are left with noise-induced hearing loss – which can be permanent.
一旦受损，这些细胞便无法对声音做出反应，进而导致噪声性听力丧失——这种损伤可能是永久性的。

9
00:00:58,210 --> 00:01:04,540
It can also leave us with a constant ringing sound in our ears, which is known as tinnitus.
此外，还可能导致耳朵里持续出现嗡嗡声，这种症状被称为耳鸣。

10
00:01:04,540 --> 00:01:07,880
So, what sounds are too loud?
那么，多大的声音才算过大呢？

11
00:01:07,880 --> 00:01:10,550
Sound is measured in decibels.
人们用分贝来衡量声音的大小。

12
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:18,160
The maximum safe level of sound humans are thought to be able to withstand without potential damage is 80db.
人类在不遭受潜在听力损伤的前提下，能够承受的最大安全音量为80分贝。

13
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:34,260
The bangs or booms of a firework display can reach 120db, the whirring mechanical sound of a food processor can be around 85db, and live music can be around 110db.
烟花绽放时的爆炸声可达120分贝，食品加工机运转时的机械嗡鸣声约为85分贝，而现场音乐的音量则大概在110分贝左右。

14
00:01:34,260 --> 00:01:41,700
If these sounds continue for a long period of time, it puts our unprotected ears at risk.
如果长时间处于这些噪音环境中，未受保护的耳朵就会面临受损风险。

15
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:44,590
So what can we do to protect our ears?
那么该如何保护我们的耳朵呢？

16
00:01:44,590 --> 00:01:53,110
Firstly, when listening to music, especially while using your headphones or earphones, make sure you keep it at a sensible volume.
首先，听音乐时，尤其是使用头戴式耳机或入耳式耳机时，一定要将音量调至合理水平。

17
00:01:53,110 --> 00:01:58,000
Also, don’t get too close to speakers that are blaring out music.
其次，不要离正在大声播放音乐的音箱太近。

18
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:03,040
And, if you go to live concerts, why not take some earbuds with you?
另外，去现场音乐会的话，不妨带上一副耳塞。

19
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:10,730
Finally, if you get a ringing sensation in your ears, it might be wise to speak to your doctor as soon as possible.
最后，如果你的耳朵出现了嗡嗡作响的情况，最好尽快去看医生。

20
00:02:10,730 --> 00:02:16,820
Your hearing is important, but there are some steps you can take to be as safe as possible.
听力至关重要，但我们可以通过一些措施，尽可能降低它受损的风险。
